NOTE: each run had exactly ONE single-frame spike (112.80ms primitives,
127.00ms sprites) not visible in the game's own HB.perf() summary (which
only aggregates a rolling 180-frame window and did not have that frame in
range at read time). Present in BOTH conditions at nearly the same
magnitude — not attributable to the sprite change under test. Not
re-isolated further (cause unknown: candidates are a one-time GC pause or
an artifact of the page.evaluate() call that starts the stress loop); see
playtest.md's perf section for how this was judged.

RE-RUN (independent second pass, same pinned build, same probe, ~2 minutes
later): the spike reproduced again in BOTH conditions at a near-identical
magnitude, confirming it is a stable artifact of this measurement's own
setup rather than a one-off fluke:

=== primitives (?sprites=0) ===
liveProjectiles: 236  liveHostiles: 10  frames sampled: 3751
p50: 0.90ms  p90: 3.10ms  p99: 3.70ms  worst: 111.80ms
frames >16.7ms: 2/3751 (0.1%)   frames >20ms: 2/3751 (0.1%)

=== sprites (shipped default) ===
liveProjectiles: 219  liveHostiles: 10  frames sampled: 3474
p50: 0.80ms  p90: 3.10ms  p99: 4.10ms  worst: 132.20ms
frames >16.7ms: 2/3474 (0.1%)   frames >20ms: 2/3474 (0.1%)

Both re-run readings land within ~1ms of the first pass at every reported
percentile except the single worst-frame outlier, which moves a bit
(112.80→111.80 primitives, 127.00→132.20 sprites) but stays in the same
~110-135ms band on both conditions both times — a single-frame artifact of
this ad hoc probe's own setup path (most likely the page.evaluate() round
trip that installs the stress loop), reproducing even at ?sprites=0 with
zero texture loads, so it is not something T-049 introduced.
